DEADLOCK

The trace describes one wait cycle.

  Coordinator state: holds manifestMu, parked in wg.Wait()
  Worker state (×8): blocked in manifestMu.Lock() inside markComplete

The cycle:

  Coordinator --(holds)--> manifestMu
  Coordinator --(waits)--> wg  --(needs Done)-->  Workers
  Workers     --(need)----> manifestMu  --(held by)--> Coordinator

Neither side can advance because:
  - Coordinator cannot reach manifestMu.Unlock() until wg.Wait() returns.
  - Workers cannot reach wg.Done() until manifestMu.Lock() succeeds.

This is lock-across-wait inversion: the coordinator holds a mutex while waiting on a wait group whose completion depends on that same mutex.

WHY 812 BEFORE THE STALL

Under the simplified pseudocode shown (manifestMu held continuously across enqueueRemaining and wg.Wait), the very first markComplete would block, so progress would be 0 — not 812. The 812 implies the real rehearsal has an asynchrony not captured in the pseudocode. The two most plausible shapes:

  (a) enqueueRemaining briefly releases manifestMu internally (e.g., between
      sub-batches), so workers that were already past their markComplete
      can finish before the coordinator re-enters the hold-and-wait phase.
  (b) Workers process the first N manifests through a fast path that does
      not require manifestMu, then switch to the lock-protected path once
      the coordinator's hold engages.

Either way, the stall mechanism is the same: once the hold-and-wait phase engages, the cycle is permanent.

MINIMAL FIX

One acquisition-site reorder. Drop the hold across wg.Wait(); the lock protects enqueueRemaining only.

Before:

  manifestMu.Lock()
  enqueueRemaining(manifests)
  wg.Wait()
  manifestMu.Unlock()

After:

  manifestMu.Lock()
  enqueueRemaining(manifests)
  manifestMu.Unlock()
  wg.Wait()

No API change. markComplete is untouched.

Safety check:
  - enqueueRemaining fully populates the work queue under the lock, so workers cannot race past it.
  - Workers consume manifests and do not re-enqueue, so no further lock-protected writes are needed after enqueue.
  - The completed map is write-only by workers; the coordinator never reads it during wg.Wait(), so the lock is not protecting any state across the wait.

REGRESSION TEST (Go)

Focused test that exercises the helpers against a synthetic shard set, runs 2000 manifests under 8 workers, and asserts progress past the historical stall point (812):

  package recoveryhelper_test

  import (
      "context"
      "fmt"
      "sync"
      "testing"
      "time"
  )

  const (
      totalManifests  = 2000
      historicalStall = 812
      workerCount     = 8
  )

  type manifest struct {
      id    string
      shard string
      work  func()
  }

  func syntheticManifests(n int) []manifest {
      out := make([]manifest, n)
      for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
          out[i] = manifest{
              id:    fmt.Sprintf("m-%04d", i+1),
              shard: fmt.Sprintf("shard-%03d", i%64),
              work:  func() { time.Sleep(50 * time.Microsecond) },
          }
      }
      return out
  }

  // rehearsal reproduces the fixed coordinator shape:
  // lock guards enqueue only, not the wg.Wait() that follows.
  func rehearsal(ctx context.Context, ms []manifest, workers int) (int, error) {
      var (
          mu        sync.Mutex
          completed = make(map[string]bool)
          queue     = make(chan manifest, 256)
          wg        sync.WaitGroup
      )
      wg.Add(len(ms))

      mu.Lock()
      for _, m := range ms {
          queue <- m
      }
      close(queue)
      mu.Unlock()

      var workerWg sync.WaitGroup
      workerWg.Add(workers)
      for i := 0; i < workers; i++ {
          go func() {
              defer workerWg.Done()
              for m := range queue {
                  m.work()
                  mu.Lock()
                  completed[m.id] = true
                  mu.Unlock()
                  wg.Done()
              }
          }()
      }
      workerWg.Wait()

      done := make(chan struct{})
      go func() { wg.Wait(); close(done) }()

      select {
      case <-done:
          return len(completed), nil
      case <-ctx.Done():
          return len(completed), ctx.Err()
      }
  }

  func TestRehearsal_ProgressesPastHistoricalStall(t *testing.T) {
      ms := syntheticManifests(totalManifests)

      ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 30*time.Second)
      defer cancel()

      completed, err := rehearsal(ctx, ms, workerCount)
      if err != nil {
          t.Fatalf("rehearsal stalled (deadlock suspected): %d/%d completed within deadline; err=%v",
              completed, totalManifests, err)
      }
      if completed <= historicalStall {
          t.Fatalf("regressed: only %d/%d completed (historical stall point was %d)",
              completed, totalManifests, historicalStall)
      }
      if completed != totalManifests {
          t.Fatalf("incomplete: %d/%d completed", completed, totalManifests)
      }
  }

